[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was marked known: one issue, one fix queued. Reading the reports, not
their subjects, shows three problems. Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October; that closed two of them.

<Callout kind="warn">
<code>parser</code> is fixed ("fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales"). <code>hint</code> won't be
fixed: the booking-v3 form is retired. <code>shift</code> is still open: neither change touches it.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {tickets} />

## The three problems

- **`parser`**: the shared parser reads every date month first, and every tenant on #4471 writes
  the day first. Dates come out with day and month swapped. Fixed.
- **`hint`**: the booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. Day-first tenants on
  booking-v3 type month first, as told, and the form reads it day first. Same symptom, different
  bug: the parser fix does not reach it. Won't fix: booking-v3 is retired.
- **`shift`**: the date moves by one day, not by a swap ("Tuesday 12 May… Monday 11 May", "only
  June", "Lisbon got Tuesday, Toronto got Monday"). The desk already read these as a one-day move
  (`desk-notes.json`). It points at a time zone conversion. Open.

## How the reports were placed

By tenant, channel and form, with `causeOf` in `causes.ts`, the same as the
[#4471 table](/kb/incidents/4471), so the counts are the desk's. A report the desk read as a
one-day move (`desk-notes.json`) stays counted where it is filed and is footnoted as `shift`.
Two things were not used:

- **Subject lines.** Every subject on a `hint` or `shift` report also appears on `parser`
  reports, so [decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject), which merges on subject
  alone, put all three problems in one ticket.
- **Intake tags.** Intake guesses from the reporter's browser. The tag disagrees with the
  tenant's cause on dozens of reports.

## Lessons

- A count is only as good as the merge. Read a sample of bodies before you merge, not after.
- Same symptom is not same cause. Merge on cause; subject is a hint, not a key.

